TI  - Learning curve, safety and diagnostic yield of the Galaxy(TM), Noah Medical Group 
      robotic bronchoscopy platform.
PG  - 361
LID - 10.21037/jtd-2025-1-2700 [doi]
LID - 361
AB  - BACKGROUND: Noah Medical Group (Reseda, CA) obtained Food and Drug Administration 
      (FDA) approval for the robotic-assisted bronchoscopy (RAB) Galaxy(TM) System in 
      2023. The platform utilizes electromagnetic navigation (EMN) and integrated 
      digital tomosynthesis (DT) for precise nodule targeting. Real-world data on its 
      performance remain limited. This study aimed to evaluate the diagnostic yield and 
      safety of the Galaxy(TM) system in the first year of its integration in an 
      academic center. METHODS: We conducted a retrospective, single-center study at an 
      academic interventional pulmonary program, reviewing RAB procedures performed 
      with the Galaxy(TM) system between February 2024 and February 2025. The primary 
      outcomes were strict diagnostic yield and safety. Secondary analyses evaluated 
      the impact of operator learning curve, biopsy modality [1.1-mm cryoprobe, 
      21-gauge fine-needle aspiration (FNA), and forceps], and nodule characteristics. 
      RESULTS: Ninety-one patients with 94 nodules (median size 2.1 cm) were included. 
      Overall diagnostic yield was 68%. Yield improved from 61% in the first six months 
      to 75% in the latter half of the study [P=0.44, odds ratio (OR) =1.289]. The 
      1.1-mm cryoprobe achieved the highest yield (80.6%), outperforming FNA (42.9%, 
      P=0.01, OR =5.543) and forceps (51.2%, P=0.046, OR =3.867). Pneumothorax occurred 
      in 13% of cases (all requiring chest tubes), and one case of self-limited massive 
      hemoptysis was reported. CONCLUSIONS: In this real-world descriptive study, the 
      Galaxy(TM) system demonstrated diagnostic performance comparable to the other EMN 
      RAB. Outcomes were influenced by a learning curve and trainee participation. 
      Routine use of the 1.1-mm cryoprobe may optimize yield.
