TI  - An unusual foreign body aspiration requiring an unusual retrieval technique.
PG  - 3098-3101
LID - 10.21037/jtd.2018.03.118 [doi]
AB  - Foreign body aspiration during medical procedures has been reported in the 
      literature. These iatrogenic incidents could be related to instruments 
      malfunction or to accidental occurrences during medical treatment. In this paper, 
      we present a report of a woman coming for a laparoscopic abdominal hysterectomy 
      who developed intraoperative bronchospasm. In an attempt to administer 
      aerosolized albuterol, the resident anesthesia provider fractured the Luer-lock 
      tip of the 60-cc syringe, which he was using to hold the albuterol nebulizer. The 
      plastic tip was dislodged into the endotracheal tube (ETT). On further inspection 
      with a fiberoptic instrument the plastic tip was located loosely adherent to the 
      distal part of the ETT and was held in place by the moisture, which had 
      precipitated in the distal tube. An intraoperative consult with interventional 
      pulmonary medicine was obtained after unsuccessfully attempting to retrieve the 
      foreign body with a grasper. The syringe tip was then removed using a Fogarty 
      balloon catheter that was threaded through the hole of the plastic tip.
